{"step_index":1599,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-02T19:49:24Z","input_tokens":2639,"cache_read_tokens":105251,"output_tokens":1032,"thinking":"The user's instruction specifically targets misinterpreting quantities or wattages as tax values, particularly when they involve two-digit numbers. The sample data contains \"20W\" in the description and a quantity of \"20\" for a second item. This confirms the need to avoid capturing such instances as tax amounts. The presence of the \"20W\" in description of Item 1 and the quantity \"20\" on Item 2 highlights a scenario that must be handled.\n\n\nFocusing on tax extraction, patterns for CGST, SGST, and IGST lines must be strictly adhered to, ignoring other numeric values. The state code comparison between supplier and buyer GSTINs determines intra-state versus inter-state scenarios. For intra-state, IGST is set to zero, with a CGST/SGST split; for inter-state, CGST and SGST are zero, with a non-zero IGST. An immediate re-audit must be triggered on quick button click for tax recalculation based on the subtotal. The buyer GSTIN is extracted from the header and raw text, using a regular expression. A possible scenario to consider is an invoice with two GSTINs.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"\"grep -rn -i \\\"buyer_gstin\\\\|customer_gstin\\\" /Users/pranit405/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/audit-it/\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":"5000","toolSummary":"\"Search for buyer_gstin or customer_gstin\""}}]}
